        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Sogdians</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Sogdiana, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> rivers, chiefly the valleys of the Zeravshan and the Kashka Darya (present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and Tajikistan), with merchant colonies from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> to the cities of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 6th century BCE, when Sogdiana appears in Persian inscriptions, to about the 11th century CE, by which time its people had become Muslim and spoke Persian or Turkic; its trade at its height in the 4th–8th centuries CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> merchants and their colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; city-states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panjikent">Panjikent</a>; wall paintings of epics, feasts and embassies; an alphabet that passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>; musicians and dancers at the Tang court</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Sogdian documents (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdian-ancient-letters">Ancient Letters</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mugh">Mount Mugh</a> archive, Buddhist, Manichaean and Christian texts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a>, the Bugut inscription); excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panjikent">Panjikent</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a> and Varakhsha; Sogdian tombs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; Persian, Greek, Chinese, Byzantine and Arabic accounts</span></div>
        </div>
