    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Sogdians, an Iranian people of the Zeravshan and Kashka Darya valleys in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and Tajikistan, lived in oasis city-states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panjikent">Panjikent</a>.</li><li>From the 4th to the 8th century CE their merchants dominated the trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, settling from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> to the Tang capital and serving Turkic and Chinese rulers.</li><li>Conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in the early 8th century, they became Muslim and Persian- or Turkic-speaking, but their alphabet lives on, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, in the Mongolian script.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A Tang history claims that Sogdian parents put honey or sugar in a newborn son’s mouth and glue in his palm, so that he would grow up to speak sweet words and to hold on to money.</p></aside>
    </section>

