    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 546–539 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> brings Sogdiana into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">329–327 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquers Sogdiana despite the resistance led by Spitamenes</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 313</span><span class="rf-text">Sogdian merchants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> write the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdian-ancient-letters">Ancient Letters</a>, found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> in 1907</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">568</span><span class="rf-text">The Sogdian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maniakh">Maniakh</a> leads a Turkic embassy to the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">mid-7th century</span><span class="rf-text">The hall of the ambassadors at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a> is painted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varkhuman">Varkhuman</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">712</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a> take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">755–763</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a>, led by a general of Sogdian and Turkic parentage, shakes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">819–999</span><span class="rf-text">Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> the Sogdians become Muslim and come to speak Persian</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sogdians"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sogdians.jpg" alt="Sogdians" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sogdian_Tribute_Bearers_on_the_Apadana_Staircase_16_(Best_Viewed_Size_%22Large%22)_(4689076272).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A.Davey from Portland, Oregon, EE UU, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sogdians were an Iranian people whose homeland, Sogdiana, lay between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>, the Oxus and Jaxartes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and Tajikistan. Its heart was the fertile valley of the Zeravshan, which the Greeks called the Polytimetus, with the oases of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panjikent">Panjikent</a>; to the south lay the valley of the Kashka Darya, with Kesh and Nakhshab. People had farmed and built settlements in these valleys long before the Sogdians are named in any text: Sarazm, in Tajikistan, goes back to the 4th millennium BCE, and Kök Tepe, near Samarkand, was occupied from at least the 15th century BCE. The Sogdians spoke an Eastern Iranian language related to Bactrian and Khwarazmian, and the Avesta, the scripture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, names the land of the Sogdians second among the good lands created by Ahura Mazda.</p><p>Sogdiana enters recorded history as a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered it, probably between 546 and 539 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> listed it among his lands in the Behistun inscription of about 520 BCE. Darius’s foundation charter at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> records that the lapis lazuli and carnelian of his palace were brought from Sogdiana, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> lists the Sogdians among the peoples who paid tribute to the Persian king and marched with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> reached Sogdiana in 329 BCE and took Maracanda, as the Greeks called Samarkand, but met there some of the fiercest resistance of his campaigns. The Sogdian noble Spitamenes destroyed a Macedonian force on the Polytimetus and fought on until his death in 328 BCE; in 327 BCE Alexander stormed the mountain fortress known as the Sogdian Rock and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxana">Roxana</a>, daughter of the Bactrian noble Oxyartes, who had been captured there. After Alexander, Sogdiana passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> and in part to the Greek kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, until nomadic peoples from the north and east overran the region in the 2nd century BCE; the Chinese envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a>, who reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> in the 120s BCE, knew it as part of Kangju. In the first centuries CE the Sogdian towns grew into small states with their own rulers and coins, writing their language in a script derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> of the Persian chancery.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mount-mugh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-mugh.jpg" alt="Mount Mugh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mount-mugh">Mount Mugh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Possible_Sogdian_King_Devashtish_or_God_Verethranga,_Aini_district,_Tajikistan,_7-8th_century_CE,_National_Museum_of_Antiquities_of_Tajikistan_(A_1060).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ALFGRN, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sogdiana was seldom united under one ruler. It was a land of oasis city-states, each a walled town with a citadel, surrounded by irrigated fields, orchards and vineyards, villages and the fortified houses of landowning nobles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> was the largest, and its ruler, who bore the title ikhshid, was usually the leading prince; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panjikent">Panjikent</a>, Kesh and Nakhshab had lords of their own. From the late 5th century the cities acknowledged in turn the overlordship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>, the Western Turks and, nominally, from the 650s, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, Sogdians took surnames from their home cities, among them Kang for Samarkand and An for Bukhara.</p><p>The best-known Sogdian town is ancient Panjikent, east of Samarkand in present-day Tajikistan, a small but flourishing town of the 5th to 8th centuries that was abandoned by the end of the 8th. Its excavators, among them Boris Marshak, who worked there for more than fifty years, uncovered streets of houses of two storeys or more, shops and workshops, two temples and a citadel. Many houses, not only those of nobles, had a reception hall with painted walls, which suggests that the wealth of trade reached well beyond the nobility.</p><p>Trade shaped the character of Sogdian society, and Chinese observers thought the Sogdians born traders: a Tang history claims that parents put honey or sugar in a newborn son’s mouth and glue in his palm, so that he would speak sweet words and hold on to money, and that their young men went abroad wherever there was profit. Sogdian documents show a society of letters and contracts. A marriage contract among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mugh">Mount Mugh</a> documents sets out the obligations of husband and wife, and one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdian-ancient-letters">Ancient Letters</a>, written in China in the early 4th century, preserves the anger of Miwnay, a woman left stranded with her daughter in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> by her husband, who told him that she would rather have been married to a dog or a pig.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manichaeism.jpg" alt="Manichaeism" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manicheans.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Most Sogdians followed a local form of the Iranian religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, which differed in many ways from the Zoroastrianism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. They kept temples of fire, and their dead were exposed and the cleaned bones gathered into ossuaries, clay boxes often decorated with figures of priests and fire altars. They also worshipped gods with their own names and images, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a>, a four-armed goddess riding a lion whose cult had come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. When Sogdian Buddhists translated their scriptures, they rendered the Indian gods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> by the names of Sogdian gods: Zurvan, Adbag and Weshparkar. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panjikent">Panjikent</a> had two temples at its centre, and the painted halls of its houses honoured the household’s own gods.</p><p>Sogdian merchants carried other faiths across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Some were Buddhists: Kang Senghui, a monk of Sogdian family born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozhi">Jiaozhi</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, translated Buddhist texts in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the 3rd century, and the largest body of surviving Sogdian literature is Buddhist, much of it translated from Chinese. Others followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a>, the religion of the Persian prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a>, and Sogdian Manichaeans converted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> ruler Bögü <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">Khagan</a> in 762 or 763, making theirs the state religion of the Uyghur Khaganate. Christians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> wrote in Sogdian too, and their texts have been found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> with Manichaean ones, most of them from the 9th and 10th centuries.</p><p>In China, Sogdian communities were overseen by officials called sabao, a title from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> for “caravan leader”, and Zoroastrian temples stood in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang’an</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and other cities. The stone funeral couches and sarcophagi of Sogdian leaders buried in northern China in the late 6th century, such as An Jia and Wirkak, show priests with cloths over their mouths tending fire altars and, on Wirkak’s sarcophagus, the soul’s passage over the Chinvat Bridge, where Zoroastrians believed the dead were judged. After the Arab conquest the Sogdians gradually became Muslims, a conversion virtually complete by the end of the 10th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afrasiab.jpg" alt="Afrasiab" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afrasiab_-_details_from_The_Ambassadors'_Painting.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sogdian art is known best from its wall paintings. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panjikent">Panjikent</a>, painting flourished from the 5th century into the 8th, and the halls of houses were covered with scenes of gods, banquets, hunts and mourning, with episodes from epics, among them the deeds of the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rustam</a>, later famous from the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>, and with fables also known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aesop">Aesop</a> and the Indian Panchatantra. Many of these paintings are now in the Hermitage Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> and the National Museum of Antiquities of Tajikistan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a>.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a>, the old site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, road builders uncovered in 1965 a hall painted in the mid-7th century for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varkhuman">Varkhuman</a>, king of Samarkand. On its main wall envoys from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaghaniyan">Chaghaniyan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Chach</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the Korean kingdom of Goguryeo come to the court, escorted by Turkic attendants, and a Sogdian inscription records the words of the envoy from Chaghaniyan; other walls show the Chinese emperor hunting, the empress in a boat and a funeral procession. At Varakhsha, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, a palace hall shows riders on elephants fighting wild beasts.</p><p>Sogdian craftsmen also carved wood and modelled clay sculpture, and Sogdian silversmiths made bowls and ewers decorated with animals and hunting scenes, which were prized from China to the steppe. In China, Sogdian musicians and dancers were famous: the music of Samarkand and that of Bukhara were among the official kinds of music at the Tang court, and the “Sogdian whirl”, a fast spinning dance, became a fashion in the capital.</p><p>The Sogdians wrote their language in an alphabet descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, and also in the scripts of the Manichaeans and of the Syriac-writing Christians, for letters, contracts, legal records, inscriptions and a large religious literature. Their language served as a common tongue of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>: the Bugut inscription, set up about 581 for the rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, is written mainly in Sogdian.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/justin-ii.jpg" alt="Justin II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solidus_of_Justin_II_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Artemide Aste, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From the 4th century CE at the latest, Sogdian merchants were the chief carriers of trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Their earliest surviving letters, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdian-ancient-letters">Ancient Letters</a>, were found by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurel-stein">Aurel Stein</a> in 1907 in an abandoned watchtower near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>. Written about 313 by Sogdians living in the towns of north-west China, they deal with business and debts and describe the disaster that struck when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, the Chinese capital, was sacked in 311. Sogdian communities grew up along the routes, in the oases of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, at Dunhuang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Wuwei</a>, in the Chinese capitals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang’an</a> and Luoyang, on the steppes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semirechye">Semirechye</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, and hundreds of Sogdian inscriptions scratched by travellers at Shatial, in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley, show them on the roads to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. They traded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, musk, horses, slaves and much else.</p><p>When the Turks built their empire on the steppe in the mid-6th century, the Sogdians, whose cities now lay within it, became its merchants, scribes and diplomats. After the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> refused to let them sell silk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, the Sogdian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maniakh">Maniakh</a> persuaded the Turkic ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istami">Istämi</a> to seek trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, and in 568 he led an embassy to the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>; the Byzantine envoy Zemarchus set out for the Turks in 569, as the historian Menander Protector records.</p><p>In China, Sogdians served the northern dynasties, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> as merchants, interpreters, officials and soldiers. The general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a>, son of a Sogdian father and a Turkic mother, rose to command the armies of the north-east and in 755 launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a>, which captured both capitals before it was put down in 763 with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>. After it, Sogdian origins became a liability in China, and many families of Sogdian descent seem to have played them down.</p><p>In the west, Arab armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in 709 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> in 712. The Sogdians rose repeatedly in the following decades, often with help from the Turgesh, a Turkic people of the steppe. Dewashtich, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panjikent">Panjikent</a>, who claimed the titles of king of Sogdiana and lord of Samarkand, fled to a fortress on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mugh">Mount Mugh</a> in 722 and, after his surrender, was crucified on the orders of the governor Sa’id al-Harashi; his archive, found in the ruins of the fortress in 1933, preserves letters and contracts in Sogdian, Arabic and Chinese.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/turfan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turfan.jpg" alt="Turfan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turpan-jiaohe-ruinas-d16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Colegota, CC BY-SA 2.5 es</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>, who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> between about 819 and 999, Sogdiana became one of the heartlands of the Islamic world, and the Samanid court at Bukhara was a cradle of New Persian literature. The Sogdians converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and in the towns their language gave way to Persian, which survives there as Tajik, and later in many places to Turkic. Sogdian religious texts were still copied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> in the 9th and 10th centuries, and around 1000 the scholar al-Biruni, from neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, recorded the Sogdian calendar and festivals; by the 11th century the Sogdians had merged into the Persian- and Turkic-speaking peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. One Sogdian dialect survives: Yaghnobi, thought to descend from the speech of the principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usrushana">Ushrusana</a>, is still spoken by the Yaghnobis of Tajikistan.</p><p>The Sogdians’ most lasting legacy was their alphabet. In the 8th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> adopted it, and from the Old Uyghur script came the traditional Mongolian script and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> script. An alphabet of the Persian chancery, carried across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> by Sogdian merchants, is thus still written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a> today.</p><p>Modern knowledge of the Sogdians was rebuilt almost entirely in the 20th century: from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdian-ancient-letters">Ancient Letters</a> and the religious texts of Turfan and Dunhuang, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mugh">Mount Mugh</a> archive, from the Soviet excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panjikent">Panjikent</a> and the discovery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a> paintings in 1965, and from the tombs of Sogdian families excavated in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, above all those found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi’an</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> since 1999. Historians such as Étienne de la Vaissière have reconstructed their network of merchant colonies, and the Sogdians, once seen as obscure go-betweens, are now recognised as the leading traders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> between the 4th and 8th centuries and as a civilisation in their own right, whose painting, music and religious life left their mark from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> to China.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Sogdians, an Iranian people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and the Zeravshan valley, appear in history in the 6th century BCE as subjects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and later as opponents of Alexander. From the 4th to the 8th century CE their merchants dominated the trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, carrying goods, faiths, music and an alphabet across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the Persian and Turkic languages absorbed them.</em></p>

